Eric Raymond offers some reasons for the anti-science, anti-progress
outlook of modern Islam:

> The early flowering of Islamic civilization, between the 7th and 10th
> centuries CE, supported a great deal of leading-edge science,
> especially in astronomy but also in mathematics and chemistry [...]
> Why didn't they follow up?  Fundamentally, their economic base was
> fragile; there's not much good cropland in North Africa, especially,
> and the Sahara has been steadily eating into that as it expanded
> throughout historical times. [...]
> Economic shrinkage encouraged, and then was exacerbated by,
> internecine warfare.  The beginning of the end was the breakup of the
> Ummaiyad caliphate into contending successor states.  After that it
> was all downhill, with science essentially proscribed by periodic
> fundamentalist revivals.

There are other reasons as well.  On the religious side Islam never
recovered from the onslaught they suffered from the Mongol Invasions of
the 13th and 14th centuries.  Before the invasions Islam had been
relatively free-thinking, and even tolerant of other beliefs, secure in
its dominant position across N.Africa, the Middle East and towards
India.  Trade flourished in the peaceful conditions that prevailed.

The Mongols allowed complete religious freedom under their rule. 
Suddenly the Islam found itself just one religion among many in the eyes
of their Mongol Overloads.  Unfortunately the Moslems, as a whole,
reacted badly to this and became very parochial.  Whenever they
succeeded in throwing off of the Mongol yoke they immediately set about
massacring their religious competitors.  This behaviour mystified the
Mongols completely and towards the end they, sadly, often found it
necessary to wipe out Moslems to preserve religious peace amongst their
subject peoples.  The effect on Moslems was to only confirm their
religious intolerances.

When the Mongol culture retreated back the anonymity of the Mongolian
steppelands it was a very different, bitter Islam that reasserted
itself.

Another very powerful reason for the instability of the power structures
in the Islamic world was the polygamy allowed by Islam.  A strong
individual would seize control in a palace coup and establish a dynasty. 
All would be fine until control passes down to the next generation,
whereupon internecine warfare breaks out between the *half*-brothers of
the dynasty.  Sociobiology and all that.  The sons of the previous ruler
by each wife would form cabals and plot against the their half-brothers. 
Result:  brief periods of peace alternating with terrible carnage.  To
ensure stability each group of full-brothers (aided by their mothers who
could only wield power through *her* sons) have to exterminate all other
bloodlines from their father.  The Wicked Uncle was a feared figure in
Islamic myth, because he was often your half-uncle.  His mother's
interests lay in exterminating *you*.

[Digression:  It was for this reason that the Mongol Empire, too,
collapsed.  Different skeins of the House of Genghis exterminated each
other in noble mongol fashion.  The Mongol Empire fragmented and the
Mongols ceased to influence history.  The Mongols have consistently got
a bad press ever since, yet they were tolerant, bought peace (Pax
Mongolia - if you didn't resist!) and opened up many trade routes. 
Their influence on history is often ignored.  They established a dynasty
in India, conquered China, the Middle East (Persia) and E.Europe and
very nearly reduced Europe to a province of Greater Mongolia - only the
return to Ulan Bator for Ogodei(?) Khan's funeral stopped the annexation
of W.Europe.  The Mongol Empire was the greatest empire the world has
ever seen - over 1/2 the world's population lay within their empire.]

Christianity, OTOH, has always regarded monogamy as the ideal.  It
refuses to recognise children born out of wedlock as legitimate heirs -
look at the problems Henry VIII had with Rome.  Charles II had loads of
bastards, but none of them succeeded to the throne.  However unjust it
may be by natural justice standards it promoted stability at the
institutional level.

