Date: Fri, 9 Apr 93 10:38:45 EDT
From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: HIST: Moslems and Mongols

(Eric Raymond's excellent, but long analysis of Islamic history sadly deleted.)

There is a just few points I would like to add and also think are relevant to
Extropianism in an historical way.

     Another reason for Islam's lack of intellectual growth was also their
greatest strength, the reverence of the Book. The Koran and Arabic served
as a great unifier of a large number of various diverse and previously
antagonistic people. For this reason, certain skills such as textual
analysis and, most importantly, skepticism never attained the prominence they
did in the Christian areas. Islamic science was brilliant in its fields which
were predominantly empirical. Any sort of knowledge that could be attained by
skillful exegesis and deduction from previous sources was valued by Islam
and they produced it.

     Eric is correct in his historical reasons as well. Besides the Mongols,
the original Arabic and Persian Islam faced challenges from Byzantium,
the Crusades, and even internally from the Turks. The coming of the Ottoman
empire also produced the end of Islamic science and learning. The fundamentally
militaristic Turks were closer to the Mongols in temperment and didn't foster
(and were unable to do so due to external threats) an enviroment conducive to
science.


 Unlike France or England which constantly strove against one another, Islam
was either supreme or sumpremely challenged. The continual wars and stuggle
 between France and England were great stimulants to advancement. Since
 neither country really faced total extermination from the other, resources
 could be devoted to the sciences. Nearly every enemy of Islam, however, was
 a massive invading force bent on total destruction. This kind of competition
 *doesn't* produce advancement. This is also similar to the Cold War. America
benefited from the challenge of Russia to leap into space and develop other
technologies, but had we actualy engaged in war, this would not have happened.
(I don't necessarily mean government were responsible for the advancements in
 either case. Private business that was stimulated by the internecine 
competition greatly benefited in both cases.)

     Why is this relevant to Extropians? Beside purely intellectual historical
interest, there are two lessons here. One, competition fosters advancement,
but extreme competition which could lead to total annhilation doesn't.  Two,
over reliance on previous learning and being bound doctrinally to a single
text or texts always hinders the advancement of knowledge. Hell, Marxists are
the single best example of this. None of these points are new to Extropians,
but historical examples are nice to confirm theory and make for interesting
discussion.

A.

