Subject: INTELL: Mental skills

> One book I read on the subject of intelligence testing emphasized the use
> of the statistical technique called factor analysis to separate out and
> identify various mental skills that go into what we generally consider
> intelligence.  In this view there is no single overall mental trait that
> represents total intelligence.  Instead, there are a wide variety of
> useful mental abilities; people who do well in many areas would be those
> we generally think of as intelligent.

On the other hand...when you *do* factor analysis on mental skills, you find
that they're all about 50% correlated and highly heritable.  The statistical
evidence suggests that there is some common enabling characteristic that is
very important.

Psychometric theorists call it "Q" or "Jensen's Q" after the person who first
pointed this out.  But nobody knows what it is.  Nerve speed is one good guess;
a personal theory of mine is that it measure the capacity level of the corpus
callosum (the main highway for information traffic between brain hemispheres).
