Date: Fri, 9 Apr 93 10:46:14 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: organizations

In a field like engineering, which is what I do, the trade-off
between self employment and working for a company are particularly
evident.  I could work as a contract engineer or start my own
engineering company.  For those who don't know, a contract engieer
essentially works for a high level temporary service, and gets
assignments typically for a few months at a time.  When the specific
task is over, your contract ends, and you go back on the market.

The risk of these choices is that you may not have work for a while,
and you need to eat regularly and pay the rent/mortgage.  The
reward is that self-employed or contract engineers make more
money when they are working, and you have the flexibility to
take extended time off when you want to.

Most people don't have a lot of savings.  Perhaps 5 percent of
households have enough assets to be financially independant, and
of those, an even smaller percentage hold assets in a form they
can draw on to live (having a lot of equity in a house doesn't
help if you still have a mortgage to pay off).  So for most people,
myself included at the present time, we choose the regularity
of a paycheck over the expected higher returns of going independant.

I have seriously considered working as a contract engineer because
of the higher pay, but I decided to wait until I had enough savings
to be financially independant, by which time my salary with a
large company such as I work for may be up in the range that
a contract engineer would earn anyway.

Dani Eder

