> for one of several strategies or sensory modes in use.  The one that indicates
> good spelling ability is visual memory retrieval ("look and say"); one of
> several ones that does *not* work is attempting to internally sound out the
> word ("phonics").

Look-Say is a much better method of teaching accurate spelling skills.
Phonics is a much better method of teaching reading skills.

Why?

When reading, phonics training allows you to produce an approximate 
pronunciation from a word represented by a small number of glyphs.
This pronunciation can then be recognized by matching it with known
words with similar pronunciations. This operation is one at which
speakers of human language excel. When writing, phonics training allows
you to produce an approximate representation in glyphs of the word
you want to write. This makes you a rotten speller.

Look-Say reading requires you to map between iconic representations
("whole words") and a word you know. This is a much harder recognition
problem. However, when you want to write the word, you go straight 
from your internal representation to an iconic ("whole word") representation.
This makes you a great speller.

The best approach is clearly to use phonics for early reading training,
and later to add a whole word approach for spelling.


