Dani Eder writes:
>For those who don't know, a contract engieer
>essentially works for a high level temporary service, and gets
>assignments typically for a few months at a time.  When the specific
>task is over, your contract ends, and you go back on the market.
>
>The risk of these choices is that you may not have work for a while,
>and you need to eat regularly and pay the rent/mortgage.  The
>reward is that self-employed or contract engineers make more
>money when they are working, and you have the flexibility to
>take extended time off when you want to.
>
>Most people don't have a lot of savings. So for most people,
>myself included at the present time, we choose the regularity
>of a paycheck over the expected higher returns of going independant.

I have been contract engineering for six years, so this message
seemed to apply to me directly; because I'm interested in the success of
fellow Extropians, I'd like to share some minor discoveries that
help to make my life a bit more stable. 

I recommend that contract engineers put aside _at least_ 10%
of every paycheck that comes in, and resolve never to touch it except under
absolute necessity.In a surprisingly short time there will be enough to
cushion
you against job loss.

Don't buy a house, and, if you can manage it, don't pay rent.
(Seriously. Real estate is a rotten investment, locally.) I've rented a 5
bedroom home
near my place of work, and split the rent by four - and rented out four of
the bedrooms.
The only time I pay is when I first set up the household, or when there's
an empty room.
(There's one now.)

Wishing you success,
